        "text": "0\nSkip to Content\nRequest A Yard Sign!\nOpen Menu Close Menu\nRequest A Yard Sign!\nOpen Menu Close Menu\n# Ryan McGraw\n### for Coastside Fire Board District D\n# Who is Ryan McGraw ?\nAs a Deputy Fire Marshal with the Menlo Park Fire Protection District, I work on the front line of fire prevention, public education, emerging technology, and community risk reduction.\nAs a Volunteer Firefighter Engineer with the Coastside Fire Protection District, I also have a direct connection to the emergency response needs of the community I call home.\nI am fortunate to regularly work alongside some of the most knowledgeable fire service professionals in the industry. I participate in drills, training events, demonstrations, and educational programs involving agencies from throughout the country. These experiences give me the opportunity to learn how other communities approach their challenges, understand what has worked—and what has not—and bring those ideas and perspectives back to the Coastside.\nMy qualifications, however, extend beyond the fire service. My experience as a live-in senior caregiver and as the father of a six-year-old has taught me the importance of listening, patience, and effective communication across generations. My daily interactions with local small-business owners, residents, contractors, and neighbors give me an understanding of community needs and concerns at a foundational level.\nI also understand emergency response from the apparatus itself. I have spent many nights on a fire engine serving our community during major emergency events. Those experiences provide a direct understanding of the personnel, equipment, infrastructure, communications, and interagency relationships that ultimately depend upon the decisions and long-term planning of the Fire Board.\nMy foundation in public service began long before my professional career. I earned the rank of Eagle Scout, received a degree in Community Public Health, spent nine years working in construction, and owned a fire protection systems business. Together with my experience in fire prevention and emergency response, these experiences have given me a well-rounded perspective on public service, business, construction, prevention, emergency operations, and the importance of community.\nI believe that combination matters. A Fire Board Director should be able to understand the operational needs of the people on the engine, the prevention challenges facing a property owner or business, the financial realities of implementing improvements, and the concerns of the residents the District exists to serve.\nMost importantly, the Coastside is not simply a district I want to represent. It is my community. I have served it from a fire engine, built a family here, worked alongside its residents, and experienced firsthand the responsibility that comes with protecting it. I would bring that same commitment, experience, and perspective to the Fire Board.\nI am the product of a successful fire board.\nI don't mean that figuratively.\nThe fact that I have been able to belong to the Coastside Volunteer Fire Department, serve this community, ride on its fire engines, and work alongside a professional paid staff is the result of decisions made long before I ever put on a uniform.\nPeople I never met sat in board meetings and made decisions about what this fire service should look like. They balanced history with progress, volunteers with paid staff, finances with public need, and somehow preserved a place for people like me to serve.\nI have benefited from that stewardship personally.\nNow I want to be responsible for it.\nOur landscape is changing, physically and metaphorically. Some of that change is necessary. Some of it isn't. I don't believe the job of a board member is to constantly find something to change. Sometimes the harder and more important job is understanding why something works, protecting it, and making sure it continues to work for the next generation.\nBecause in the fire service, the end result of all of this is actually pretty simple:\nA big red fire engine needs to show up when somebody is having the worst day of their life.\nI have sat in that fire engine.\nAnd when you're sitting there, heading to someone's house at two in the morning, you realize just how many things had to go right before you ever turned the key.\nSomeone had to answer the phone.\nSomeone had to build and maintain the dispatch system that told us where to go.\nSomeone had to give that property an address.\nSomeone had to build the road we're driving on, put up the street sign we're looking for, maintain the water system we're going to need, and install the traffic-preemption system clearing intersections ahead of us.\nSomeone had to establish the staffing that put us on that engine.\nSomeone had to buy the engine.\nSomeone had to maintain it.\nAnd the people sitting inside it collectively spent thousands of hours learning what to do when we arrive.\nMeanwhile, before we ever get there, a smoke alarm may have already given a family enough warning to get outside. A building inspector, contractor, fire inspector, engineer, or sprinkler fitter may have made a decision years earlier that changes what happens that night.\nNone of those things are particularly dramatic by themselves.\nTogether, they are the fire service.\nThat is what I think about when I think about serving on the Fire Board.\nNot just fire engines. Not just budgets. Not just contracts, buildings, equipment, or response times.\nI think about the thousands of seemingly ordinary decisions that eventually become someone's emergency response.\nI've spent enough nights on the fire engine to see the end result. I spend my professional life in fire prevention seeing another side of the same system. And I live here, where the decisions being made aren't abstract to me. They affect my neighbors, my family, the firefighters I serve beside, and the community that gave me the opportunity to become part of its fire service in the first place.\nI believe in each pillar of that system.\nI also know I didn't build it.\nI inherited it.\nMy responsibility is to understand it, protect what works, improve what needs improving, and eventually hand it to someone else better than I found it.\nI owe that to this community.\nThis is the way I best know how to give back.\n### Built on Service. Backed by Community.\n#### Prevent.\n#### Prepare.\n#### Partner.\n# The Coastside Fire Board\nCFPD is an independent special district, governed by a five-member Board of Directors. Directors serve four-year terms and currently represent geographic Districts A through E.\nCFPD changed to district-based elections in 2022. Historically, the board had nine members in 2007, seven beginning in 2009, and was reduced to five in 2011.\nThe Board’s major functions include developing District policy, financial responsibilities, strategic planning, and oversight of District operations and contracts.\nThe Board ratifies annual budgets, audits and expenditures and has authority to enter into contracts on behalf of CFPD.\nCFPD has contracted with CAL FIRE since 2008 to provide operational staffing and management.\nThe current major CAL FIRE agreement runs from July 1, 2020 through June 30, 2027.\nCFPD itself remains responsible for costs associated with property and apparatus needed for CAL FIRE personnel to perform their work.\nNormal daily operational staffing are three three-person ALS engine companies, operating from Half Moon Bay, El Granada and Moss Beach, plus a Battalion Chief.\nThe District is funded predominantly through property-tax revenue and its property-tax rate has not been increased in 40 years.\nRegular Board meetings are normally held on the fourth Wednesday of each month at 4:00 p.m. at District Headquarters, 1191 Main Street, Half Moon Bay.\nRegular agendas are posted 72 hours in advance and special-meeting agendas 24 hours in advance under the cited Brown Act provisions.\nCFPD currently lists four Board committees: Finance, Volunteer, Land and Structures, and Vegetation Management.\nCFPD received District of Distinction accreditation in 2016 and 2020 and a District of Transparency accreditation in 2020.\nEndorsements\n## Suzie Morasci\nHalf Moon Bay City Council Candidate District 5\n## Bev Cunha Ashcraft\nBusiness Owner\n## Breanna Lafontaine\nCUSD School Board Trustee\n## Andrea Rosenthal\nCUSD School Board Candidtate District E\n## Kristen McGraw\nHatch PTO President - Super Mom\n## Tito Hernandez\nVice Chair, Half Moon Bay Recreation Comission\n## Pete Cerneka\nCabrillo Unified School District Board Member\n## Chad Fairbrother\nVolunteer Fire Captain Coastside Fire Protection District & Half Moon Bay Resident\n## Daniella and Chris Romeo\nSan Francisco Firefighter/Coastside Volunteer Firefighter and Volunteer Fire Captain Coastside Fire Protection District - Coastside Business Owners\n## Cris Serrano\nSMC Sheriffs Sergeant and Half Moon Bay Resident\n## Ellen Chiri\nCoastside Historian\n## Misty Veloso\nCUSD Educator\n## Ari Delay\nRetired Fire Chief, Retired Coastside Battalion Chief, Current La Honda Fire Chief\n## Lau Hodges\nLocal Business Owner ‘Meadow On Main’\n## Eric Dooley\nHalf Moon Bay Resident -Firm District D Advocate\n## Justin Stockman\nFire Captain Santa Clara County Fire & Half Moon Bay Resident\n## Neily Hunt\nFire Captain Menlo Park Fire & Half Moon Bay Resident\n# Ryan McGraw for Fire Board\nRyan McGraw for Fire Board\n### Useful Coastside Links\n#### If you are having an emergency dial or text 911.\n#### Coastside Services\nAlas\nCoastside Hope\nConsulate General of Mexico, SF\n#### Current Rain Status\nClick Here for Rain\n#### Current Wind Status\nClick Here for Wind\n#### Current Air Quality Status\nClick Here for Air Quality\n#### Current Fire Status\nClick here for Fire Status\n#### Current Power Status\nClick Here for Power Status\n#### Unhoused & Mental Health Services\nClick here to get County Services\nClick Here for SMC Connect\n#### Useful Smartphone Apps\nWatch Duty - Fire Tracking and Information\nPulsepoint - Current, Local Emergency Reporting",
